    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Uke-Shogi (Imo Shogi, 芋将棋) was a Japanese card game popular in the late 19th and early 20th century. It was documented in 1927 under the name Imo Shogi in the judicial survey Tobaku ni kansuru chôsa (A Research into Gambling). The game used a deck of 40 or 42 cards representing Shogi pieces (Kings, Rooks, Bishops, Golds, Silvers, Knights, Lances, Pawns) and is considered a possible ancestor of Goita. Surviving descriptions are brief and ambiguous; the game is no longer played.',
